---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import data from '../data/tribe_conversion.json';

// Mechanic transcribed from Tile.doTribeTurn (Tile.cs ~11372) and
// Game.findNearestSettlementTribe (Game.cs:15111). Numbers from tribeLevel.xml
// via build_tribe_conversion.py.
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;
const { levels, search, adopters, barbarian, counts } = data as any;
const converting = levels.filter((l: any) => l.canConvert);
const milestones = converting[0]?.odds.map((o: any) => o.turnsAfter) ?? [];
---

<Base
  title="Barbarian Site Conversion"
  active="tribe-conversion"
  pageMark="🏕️"
  description="How barbarian sites become tribal sites in Old World. From a set turn onward, each barbarian settlement rolls a few percent per turn to be adopted by a tribe that has a settlement within 12 tiles, and the roll only succeeds when no player unit and no damaged barbarian is nearby. Includes start turns and per-turn odds by tribe level, transcribed from Tile.doTribeTurn."
  pageStats={[
    { label: 'levels that convert', value: `${counts.convertingLevels} of ${levels.length}` },
    { label: 'to nearest settlement', value: `${search.range} tiles` },
    { label: 'adopting tribes', value: counts.adopters },
  ]}
>
  <p class="lede">
    A barbarian camp does not stay barbarian forever. Once a game is past a certain turn, every
    barbarian settlement rolls each turn to be <b>adopted by a nearby real tribe</b>, which hands
    the site to that tribe and turns the barbarians standing on it into that tribe's soldiers. It
    happens rarely, because the roll is only a few percent and the site has to be <em>quiet</em>
    at that moment. The rules are transcribed from <code>Tile.doTribeTurn</code> and
    <code>Game.findNearestSettlementTribe</code>, and the numbers come from
    <code>tribeLevel.xml</code>.
  </p>

  <section class="tc">
    <h2 class="tc__h">The roll, step by step</h2>
    <ol class="tc__steps">
      <li><b>Only barbarian sites are eligible.</b> The site's tribe must be one that
        <em>cannot</em> do diplomacy, meaning {barbarian.map((b: any) => b.name).join(', ')}. A
        site already held by a real tribe never rolls again.</li>
      <li><b>The clock has to have started.</b> Nothing happens until the turn passes the tribe
        level's start turn (below). Before that, the check is skipped entirely.</li>
      <li><b>Roll the percent</b>, once per site per turn.</li>
      <li><b>Find an adopter.</b> Two different distances are involved. To <em>qualify</em>, a
        tribe must own a settlement within <b>{search.range} tiles</b> of this site in straight
        line hex distance, which ignores terrain and so counts across mountains and water. Among
        the tribes that qualify, the site goes to the one whose settlement is closest by actual
        <b>pathfinding</b> over land or water. With no qualifying tribe there is no
        conversion.</li>
      <li><b>The site must be quiet.</b> The game scans every tile within
        <b>{converting[0]?.unitRange ?? 3}</b> of the site, and it cancels the conversion for that
        turn when <em>any</em> player unit is present, or when any barbarian of that site is
        <em>damaged</em>.</li>
      <li><b>The handover.</b> The site becomes the new tribe's, trade networks rebuild, and each
        barbarian in range is recreated under the new tribe. A barbarian that has a city it can
        strike from where it stands becomes a <b>raider</b> against that city instead.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2 class="tc__h">When it can happen, and how likely</h2>
    <p class="tc__intro">
      Both the start turn and the chance per turn come from the game's <b>Tribe Level</b>
      setting. The cumulative columns give the odds that one undisturbed site has converted after
      that many eligible turns, which is <code>1 − (1 − p)ⁿ</code>.
    </p>
    <div class="grid"><div class="grid__scroll">
      <table class="ntbl tc__tbl">
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th class="rowlabel rowlabel--corner">Tribe level</th>
            <th class="nhdr nhdr--num">Starts after turn</th>
            <th class="nhdr nhdr--num">Chance / turn</th>
            {milestones.map((n: number) => (
              <th class="nhdr nhdr--num">after {n} turns</th>
            ))}
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {levels.map((l: any) => (
            <tr class={l.canConvert ? '' : 'tc__off'}>
              <td class="rowlabel tc__name">{l.name}</td>
              {l.canConvert ? (
                <>
                  <td class="cell cell--num">{l.startTurn}</td>
                  <td class="cell cell--num tc__pct">{l.percent}%</td>
                  {l.odds.map((o: any) => (
                    <td class="cell cell--num">{o.chance}%<span class="tc__sub">by turn {o.byTurn}</span></td>
                  ))}
                </>
              ) : (
                <td class="cell cell--num tc__never" colspan={2 + milestones.length}>never converts</td>
              )}
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div></div>
    <p class="tc__note">
      Higher tribe levels convert <b>earlier and faster</b>, and Raging starts 30 turns before
      Weak does and rolls twice as often. On <b>Passive</b>, and with tribes off, sites never
      convert at all, which is why you can play for hundreds of hours without meeting the
      mechanic.
    </p>

    <h2 class="tc__h">Who can adopt a site</h2>
    <p class="tc__intro">
      Only the {counts.adopters} tribes you can negotiate with, meaning the ones with a
      <code>bDiplomacy</code> flag, can adopt a site. Each one must already have a settlement of
      its own within range to reach out from.
    </p>
    <div class="tc__tribes">
      {adopters.map((t: any) => (
        <span class="tc__tribe">{t.name}</span>
      ))}
    </div>
    <h2 class="tc__h">Why the adopter can look far away</h2>
    <p class="tc__intro">
      The {search.range} tiles are measured to <b>the tribe's nearest settlement</b> rather than
      to wherever you think that tribe lives. The two points below follow from that, and together
      they explain conversions that look impossible.
    </p>
    <ul class="tc__why">
      <li><b>Tribes own scattered settlements.</b> A tribe's main cluster can sit 30 tiles away
        while one outlying camp of theirs sits 9 tiles from the site being converted. You see the
        name and colour of a distant tribe, and the game measured to the near camp.</li>
      <li><b>Conversions chain.</b> Handing over a site registers it as a settlement of the new
        tribe (<code>Tile.changeImprovement</code> calls <code>addSettlement</code>), and the new
        settlement is itself a valid anchor, so it can bring the <em>next</em> barbarian camp
        {search.range} tiles further out into range. A tribe can cross the map one adoption at a
        time and end up owning sites nowhere near where it started.</li>
    </ul>

    <p class="tc__note">
      In practice a barbarian camp near an established tribe will probably convert sooner or
      later, and when it does you gain a neighbour you can negotiate with through trade, truce and
      alliance, where before there was only a permanent enemy. Parking a unit next to the camp
      blocks the conversion for as long as it stays, and so does leaving one of the camp's
      defenders wounded.
    </p>

    <p class="tc__see">
      See also <a href={`${base}tribes`}>Tribes</a> and
      <a href={`${base}camp-spawning`}>Camp Unit Spawning</a>.
    </p>
  </section>
</Base>

<style>
  .lede { max-width: 88ch; color: var(--text-dim); font-size: 0.86rem; line-height: 1.6; margin: 0 0 1.4rem; }
  .lede b { color: var(--text); }
  .lede code, .tc code { font-family: var(--font-mono); font-size: 0.85em; background: var(--bg-elev);
    border: 1px solid var(--border); border-radius: 4px; padding: 0.03rem 0.3rem; color: var(--text-soft); }

  .tc__h { font-family: var(--font-display); font-size: 1.1rem; color: var(--accent); margin: 1.6rem 0 0.5rem; }
  .tc__h:first-of-type { margin-top: 0; }
  .tc__intro, .tc__note { max-width: 88ch; font-size: 0.82rem; color: var(--text-dim); line-height: 1.6; margin: 0 0 0.8rem; }
  .tc__note { color: var(--text-faint); margin-top: 0.7rem; }
  .tc__intro b, .tc__note b { color: var(--text); }

  .tc__steps { max-width: 88ch; margin: 0 0 0.6rem; padding-left: 1.2rem; display: grid; gap: 0.45rem;
    font-size: 0.84rem; color: var(--text-dim); line-height: 1.55; }
  .tc__steps b { color: var(--text); }

  .tc__tbl { max-width: 62rem; }
  .tc__name { text-align: left; font-family: var(--font-display); font-size: 0.9rem; color: var(--text); }
  .nhdr--num, .tc__tbl :global(.cell--num) { text-align: right; }
  .tc__tbl :global(.cell--num) { font-family: var(--font-mono); font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;
    font-size: 0.84rem; vertical-align: top; }
  .tc__pct { color: var(--gold); }
  .tc__sub { display: block; font-family: var(--font-body); font-size: 0.58rem; color: var(--text-faint); }
  .tc__never { color: var(--text-faint); font-style: italic; text-align: left !important; }
  .tc__off .tc__name { color: var(--text-faint); }

  .tc__why { max-width: 88ch; margin: 0 0 0.4rem; padding-left: 1.1rem; display: grid; gap: 0.45rem;
    font-size: 0.82rem; color: var(--text-dim); line-height: 1.55; }
  .tc__why b { color: var(--text); }
  .tc__tribes { display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: 0.35rem; margin: 0 0 0.4rem; }
  .tc__tribe { font-family: var(--font-mono); font-size: 0.72rem; color: var(--text-soft);
    background: var(--bg-elev); border: 1px solid var(--border); border-radius: 4px; padding: 0.15rem 0.5rem; }

  .tc__see { margin-top: 1.4rem; font-size: 0.82rem; color: var(--text-dim); }
  .tc__see a { color: var(--accent); }
</style>
